{
  "data": {
    "text": "Resting-State Functional Connectivity and Network Analysis of Cerebellum with Respect to IQ and Gender\n\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\npublished: 26 April 2017\ndoi: 10.3389/fnhum.2017.00189\nFrontiers in Human Neuroscience | Volume 11 | Article 189\nCitation:\nPezoulas VC, Zervakis M, Michelogiannis S and Klados MA (2017) Resting-State Functional Connectivity and Network Analysis of Cerebellum with Respect to IQ and Gender.\nFront. Hum. Neurosci. 11:189. doi: 10.3389/fnhum.2017.00189\n\nSubjects\nOur data was collected from the Human Connectome Project (HCP) database. Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) data was initially acquired from 492 healthy subjects at rest with eyes open with relaxed fixation on a projected bright cross-hair on a dark background. All subjects with psychiatric history, extensive substance use and hard alcohol history have been removed. Additional information related to siblings and twins have been obtained, and the population has been restricted to only one member of a sibling/twin pair. Fluid IQ scores were obtained per subject prior to scanning. Subjects were separated based on their fluid IQ scores into two groups as described in the following section.\n\nIQ Groups Formation\nIn this study, IQ score is defined as the number of correct responses per subject on an abbreviated Raven’s patterns test. To define low and high-IQ groups, we used the distribution from all 492 subjects. The low-IQ group includes 69 subjects, whereas the high-IQ group is composed of 67 subjects; the mid-IQ subjects were discarded so that our population of interest consists of 136 well-separated subjects (69 low-IQ/67 high-IQ). There are 25 males and 44 females in the low-IQ group, while 29 males and 38 females are involved in the high-IQ group. Ages are provided in intervals: 22–25 (17/9), 26–30 (24/32), 31–35 (28/25), and one subject older than 36 years (high-IQ group).\n\nResting State fMRI Data\nResting-state BOLD fMRI data were obtained through a gradient-echo EPI sequence from a 3T scanner (91 volumes; TR = 720 ms, TE = 33.1 ms, FA = 52°, FOV = 208 × 180 mm, slice thickness = 2.0 mm; 2.0 mm isotropic voxels). Pre-processed BOLD time-series (15 min duration, 1,200 frames) were acquired from the selected 136 subjects. HCP investigators performed preprocessing and denoising using FSL’s MELODIC and FIX, as well as volume- and surface-based pipelines.\n\nPreprocessing\nThe first volume-based pipeline removes spatial distortions, realigns volumes using FSL’s FLIRT-based motion correction, normalizes the intensity of 4D images to a global mean, registers data into MNI space and finally masks the data with the final brain mask derived from FreeSurfer segmentation. The second surface-based pipeline transforms the time series to CIFTI gray-ordinates space with 2 mm average surface vertex and subcortical spacing. Surface data was smoothed using a 2 mm FWHM kernel and ICA components were classified with FIX to reconstruct de-noised signals.\n\nGraph Analysis\nCerebellum was parcellated into 28 lobules/ROIs using the SUIT atlas; Vermis Crus I was excluded. Functional connectivity was assessed by computing Pearson’s correlation coefficients between each pair of the 27 ROIs; negative correlations were discarded from further analysis. Minimum Spanning Trees (MSTs) were constructed using Kruskal’s algorithm and global/local metrics were computed. Statistical analysis used 1-way unbalanced ANOVA with FDR correction (α = 0.05) to compare (i) low vs high IQ, (ii) males within IQ groups, and (iii) females within IQ groups.\n\nResults (selected)\nCerebellum manifests a small-world network structure in both IQ populations (low-IQ: 1.2644 ± 0.1765; high-IQ: 1.2126 ± 0.1010). Significant differences between low and high-IQ groups were found for average clustering coefficient, connectivity, diameter and radius. In females, additional differences included small-worldness. Hubs included Left VI (highest betweenness and degree in 36–52% of subjects across groups).\n\nEthics Statement\nThe HCP consent procedure was approved by the Washington University institutional review board. Data use followed open-access terms of the Human Connectome Project.\n\nAvailability & Publisher\nFull-text: Frontiers in Human Neuroscience (CC BY). DOI: 10.3389/fnhum.2017.00189. Publisher: Frontiers."
